---
/**
 * Faq — block 11. Filters the `faq` collection (already populated by an
 * earlier task, see src/content/faq) to entries whose `surfaces` array
 * contains the given `surface`, sorts by `order`, and takes the first
 * `limit`.
 *
 * Parameterised (Task 1, core-pages) so the same flat-list rendering
 * serves the homepage (`surface="home"`, the default) and `/pricing`
 * (`surface="pricing"`). `/faq` needs a grouped-by-category layout instead
 * — a different layout, not a parameter — and reuses only the `<details>`
 * item rendering, not this component. Every prop's default is this
 * component's previous hardcoded value, so an unparameterised call
 * (`<Faq />`, as the homepage still makes) renders byte-identical output.
 *
 * Rendered as native <details>/<summary> — not a hand-rolled accordion.
 * Native disclosure needs zero JavaScript, is keyboard-operable (Enter /
 * Space toggle a <summary> for free), and is announced correctly by
 * screen readers without any ARIA bookkeeping on our part. The default
 * disclosure triangle is removed and replaced with a custom plus/minus
 * indicator that flips purely off the `[open]` attribute selector — no
 * script is involved in that either.
 *
 * Also emits a FAQPage JSON-LD block for the same entries (Task 15 fix
 * round 1, master brief §50), unless `emitSchema` is false — for a page
 * that renders FAQs twice, or a grouped layout emitting its own, so two
 * FAQPage blocks never land on one page. The script tag is placed
 * in-section rather than via BaseLayout's `slot="head"` — a `slot="head"`
 * attribute set inside this component's own template would not forward up
 * through <BaseLayout><Faq /></BaseLayout> since Faq isn't given any
 * children to slot in the first place. Google's structured-data guidance
 * permits JSON-LD anywhere in <head> or <body>, so this is valid as-is.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FIXED 2026-08-16: MULTI-PARAGRAPH ANSWERS RAN TOGETHER, AND ANSWER LINKS
 * RENDERED A DIFFERENT COLOUR HERE THAN ON /faq.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * global.css resets `* { margin: 0 }`, and the only paragraph rules this
 * file carried were `p:first-child { margin-top: 0 }` and `p:last-child
 * { margin-bottom: 0 }` — which zero margins that were already zero.
 * Nothing restored spacing BETWEEN blocks, so an answer with more than one
 * rendered as an unbroken block. Measured in Chrome at 320px on the live
 * homepage: both paragraphs of faq/cost.md computed 0px. Separately, a link
 * inside an answer rendered rgb(4,106,108) with a bordered underline on
 * /faq and rgb(77,77,77) with a plain underline here — the same answer, two
 * link treatments, decided by which page quoted it.
 *
 * Task 9 found the spacing half while building /faq and deliberately did
 * not fix it, because the standing ruling for that task was that / and
 * /pricing come out byte-identical from a full-file diff of dist/. It added
 * rules to FaqGrouped.astro instead, for /faq alone — which is what created
 * the link divergence. THAT RULING HAS SINCE BEEN NARROWED BY THE PERSON
 * WHO MADE IT: the byte-identical bar existed to stop an EXTRACTION
 * REFACTOR silently altering live pages, not to freeze this component
 * against a deliberate correction.
 *
 * A NOTE THAT USED TO SIT HERE SAID THE TWO SURFACES DIFFERED "IN ONE
 * RESPECT". That was wrong — there were three (block margins, `li + li`,
 * and the link rules) — and being wrong is why it is worth recording: a
 * hand-written claim about how far a divergence extends is exactly the kind
 * that goes stale the next time somebody adds to one side of it.
 *
 * THE FIX IS NOT IN THIS FILE. All four surfaces that render a content
 * collection's markdown through <Content /> — this one, FaqGrouped.astro,
 * MeetHelpers.astro's helper bio and Reviews.astro's review quote — carried
 * the same two useless `:global` rules and the same missing block spacing.
 * The treatment now lives once, as `.rich-text` in src/styles/global.css,
 * and each surface opts in by putting the class on the element it hands to
 * <Content />. See that file for the whole argument; the short version is
 * that four copies of a rule is why there were four copies of a bug.
 *
 * WHAT CHANGES ON THE TWO LIVE PAGES, stated because this is an intended
 * change and the diff must be visible rather than discovered: every block
 * after an answer's first gains --space-4 above it, list items gain
 * --space-2 between them, and a link inside an answer becomes accent-
 * coloured with a bordered underline instead of inheriting the muted body
 * colour. Nothing outside `.faq-answer` moves.
 *
 * The list RESET is deliberately untouched. `ul, ol { list-style: none;
 * padding: 0 }` in global.css means a bulleted answer still renders as
 * unmarked stacked lines rather than as a bulleted list, here and on /faq
 * alike. That is a decision about the site's list treatment, it affects
 * markup far beyond this component, and this fix does not smuggle it in.
 */
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'
import Container from '../components/Container.astro'
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro'
import { faqPageSchema, markdownToPlainText } from '../lib/structured-data'

interface Props {
  surface?: 'home' | 'faq' | 'pricing'
  limit?: number
  emitSchema?: boolean
  heading?: string
  lede?: string
}

const { surface = 'home', limit = 6, emitSchema = true, heading, lede } = Astro.props

const filteredFaqs = (await getCollection('faq'))
  .filter((entry) => entry.data.surfaces.includes(surface))
  .sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)
  .slice(0, limit)

const rendered = await Promise.all(
  filteredFaqs.map(async (entry) => ({
    entry,
    Content: (await entry.render()).Content,
  })),
)

// Structured data reuses the same filtered entries as the visible list,
// converting each entry's raw markdown body to plain text for
// acceptedAnswer.text. This does not affect the <Content /> markdown
// rendering below.
const faqSchema = emitSchema
  ? faqPageSchema(
      filteredFaqs.map((entry) => ({
        question: entry.data.question,
        answer: markdownToPlainText(entry.body ?? ''),
      })),
    )
  : null
---

<section class="faq">
  {emitSchema && <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />}
  {/*
    Container `default` (72rem), NOT `narrow` (48rem), with the reading
    measure applied to `.faq-column` inside it.

    A narrow Container centres its 768px column inside the viewport, so at
    1920px this block's left edge sat at x=598 while every other section
    heading on the page started at x=408 — a 190px indent, with
    left-aligned text inside it, reading as a mistake rather than a
    decision. Narrowing the measure is the right instinct; centring it is
    what broke the page's left axis. FinalCta uses `narrow` correctly
    because it is `text-align: center` throughout, so its column has no
    left axis to disagree with.
  */}
  <Container>
    <div class="faq-column">
    {/*
      The lede carries no frequency claim. "The things we're asked most
      often" ranks questions by how often they arrive, and nobody has that
      data — the same defect as the two claims W-6 deleted from /pricing,
      and the third found while sweeping for them. This default renders on
      the homepage, so leaving it would have shipped a claim this branch
      removed three times elsewhere. Restore one only when DirectHired
      supplies what families actually ask (live audit, C3).
    */}
    <SectionHeader
      title={heading ?? 'Questions families ask us'}
      lede={lede ?? 'A few answers to begin with. See the full list for everything else.'}
    />

    <div class="faq-list">
      {
        rendered.map(({ entry, Content }) => (
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary class="faq-question">
              <span>{entry.data.question}</span>
              <span class="faq-icon" aria-hidden="true" />
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer rich-text">
              <Content />
            </div>
          </details>
        ))
      }
    </div>

      <p class="faq-more">
        <a class="faq-link" href="/faq">See all FAQs</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  .faq {
    padding-block: var(--space-section);
    /* White, between cream neighbours (10b Reviews above — or 09
       Services, while 10a/10b render nothing — and 12 Final CTA below).
       No Card in this block, so white is free here. */
    background: var(--color-surface-raised);
  }

  /* The reading measure, matched to Container's `narrow` (48rem) but left-
     aligned inside the default container instead of centred in the
     viewport, so the FAQ's left edge lands on the same axis as every
     other section heading on the page. */
  .faq-column {
    max-width: 48rem;
  }

  .faq-list {
    margin-top: var(--space-12);
  }

  .faq-item {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .faq-item:first-child {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .faq-question {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-block: var(--space-6);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Remove the native disclosure marker so the custom indicator is the only one. */
  .faq-question::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-question::marker {
    content: '';
  }

  .faq-question:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  .faq-icon {
    position: relative;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    width: 1.25rem;
    height: 1.25rem;
  }

  .faq-icon::before,
  .faq-icon::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    background: var(--color-accent);
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease);
  }

  .faq-icon::before {
    width: 12px;
    height: 2px;
  }

  .faq-icon::after {
    width: 2px;
    height: 12px;
  }

  .faq-item[open] .faq-icon::after {
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scaleY(0);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    max-width: 60ch;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-6);
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
  }

  /*
   * NO :global() PROSE RULES HERE, AND THAT IS THE FIX. The block rhythm
   * and the link treatment for everything <Content /> renders come from
   * `.rich-text` in src/styles/global.css, which the answer div carries.
   * This file styles the CONTAINER — measure, colour, face, padding — and
   * nothing inside it. See global.css for why four copies of a `:global`
   * rule turned into four copies of a bug.
   */
  .faq-more {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .faq-link {
    /* `relative` so the hit-area pseudo-element below is positioned
       against the link rather than the page. */
    position: relative;
    font-family: var(--font-text);
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }

  /*
   * The tap target, extended to 44px without moving the underline.
   *
   * This link rendered 20px tall — less than half the comfortable touch
   * target, and it is the only navigational link inside <main> on several
   * pages. Padding would have worked but would have dropped the
   * border-bottom 12px below the text, turning the underline into a rule.
   * A transparent overlay extends the hit area instead and leaves the type
   * exactly where it is. `.faq-more` has --space-8 of margin above it, so
   * the overlay cannot reach the last FAQ item's summary.
   */
  .faq-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    inset-block: -12px;
  }

  .faq-link:hover {
    color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }
</style>
